# The Prolog backend's facts for binary, a knowledge module of their own (one
# set of backend facts per backend, as fixed_width_prolog_backend.pl is for
# fixed_width): the canonical binary naturals of binary_set P are
# represented natively, each as one integer (the family binary_natural,
# gezi.jit.BinaryNaturalFamily), and the three constructors the codec
# decodes into and encodes from are bound to it, and are leaves, so that no
# round unfolds them and the refinement keeps them as written. A filter
# `x ∥ binary_set P`, P a boolean written out, in a term is then a decode
# point of the JIT, P read from that syntax. Trusted, as every native
# specialization is; the decoding law (BinaryNaturalDecodingLawTest) checks
# the JIT against weak_head on generated numerals, canonical and not, under
# both sets, and the backend's check (BackendChecks) checks the
# constructors' shape and parameters when the library is compiled. No lemma
# justifies these facts. Compiled with binary and native_types.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega corpora "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero binary_even binary_odd _ binary_set
. export
  ( λ _ _ _ backend
    . backend
    λ _ _ representations _ _ _
    . representations
    λ s
    . s binary_set λk. k "binary_natural"
  + λ _ rewriting _ _
    . rewriting
    λ _ _ leaves
    . leaves
      ( λk. k binary_odd
      + λk. k binary_even
      + λk. k binary_zero
      )
  + λ _ _ _ backend
    . backend
    λ _ natives _ _ _ _
    . natives
      ( λs. s binary_odd λk. k "binary_odd"
      + λs. s binary_even λk. k "binary_even"
      + λs. s binary_zero λk. k "binary_zero"
      )
  )
